{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-a46fce7afd6fb596", "type": "tool_result", "content": "# HoliTok:A Coutinuous Holistic Tokenization with Robust Dual Capabilities of Speech Generation and Understanding\narXiv: 2605.29948\nAuthors: \nYear: 2026 | Venue: \nTopic: \nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. Proposal of HoliTok, a continuous holistic speech tokenizer that encodes 48kHz speech into a compact 25Hz sequence of 128-dimensional latents for unified generation and understanding.\n  2. Development of a progressive training strategy that sequentially establishes high-fidelity waveform reconstruction, regularizes the latent distribution via a variational bottleneck, and enriches the space with semantic distillation and task-aware supervision.\n  3. Construction of a unified AR+DiT downstream architecture that leverages the shared HoliTok latent sequence to jointly support continuous speech generation and understanding.\n  4. Empirical demonstration that HoliTok achieves competitive reconstruction fidelity, enhanced generative learnability, and robust dual capabilities in unified modeling without requiring additional optimization tricks.\n===\n# HoliDubber: Holistic Video Dubbing for Complex Acoustic Scenes via Text-Guided Audio Synthesis\narXiv: 2606.09098\nAuthors: Wenhao Guan, Yifan Duan, Junxi Liu, Yu Gu, Feng Dang, Kaidi Wang, Qingyang Hong, Lin Li, Xie Chen\nYear: 2026 | Venue: \nTopic: \nStatus: unread\n===\n# On the Distillation Loss Functions of Speech VAE for Unified Reconstruction, Understanding, and Generation\narXiv: 2604.12383\nAuthors: Changhao Cheng, Wei Wang, Wangyou Zhang, Dongya Jia, Jian Wu, Zhuo Chen, Yanmin Qian\nYear: 2026 | Venue: \nTopic: codec_vae\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. Systematic exploration of different distillation loss functions for speech VAEs aligned with foundation models across reconstruction, understanding, and generation tasks.\n  2. Introduction of a dimension-wise distillation scheme that captures temporal structure and local feature variations.\n  3. Formulation of a joint-marginal alignment approach that enforces intra-distribution consistency between VAE latents and foundation model features via frame-level and sequence-level losses.\n  4. Development of an adaptive weighting strategy that dynamically balances reconstruction and alignment losses based on gradient norms.\n  5. Demonstration that the joint-marginal alignment with adaptive weighting achieves the best overall balance and performance across reconstruction, understanding, and generation.\n  6. Analysis revealing that frame-wise alignment primarily captures semantic information for understanding, while sequence-level alignment preserves acoustic information for reconstruction and generation.\n\nTags: 压缩, 压缩/量化-VAE, SSL", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "6fe9f359-dca2-4a37-92e1-5115245d58de", "uuid": "b09164b0-4286-4ef9-9cdf-6f42c3a76eae", "timestamp": "2026-08-04T09:29:16.164Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": "# HoliTok:A Coutinuous Holistic Tokenization with Robust Dual Capabilities of Speech Generation and Understanding\narXiv: 2605.29948\nAuthors: \nYear: 2026 | Venue: \nTopic: \nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. Proposal of HoliTok, a continuous holistic speech tokenizer that encodes 48kHz speech into a compact 25Hz sequence of 128-dimensional latents for unified generation and understanding.\n  2. Development of a progressive training strategy that sequentially establishes high-fidelity waveform reconstruction, regularizes the latent distribution via a variational bottleneck, and enriches the space with semantic distillation and task-aware supervision.\n  3. Construction of a unified AR+DiT downstream architecture that leverages the shared HoliTok latent sequence to jointly support continuous speech generation and understanding.\n  4. Empirical demonstration that HoliTok achieves competitive reconstruction fidelity, enhanced generative learnability, and robust dual capabilities in unified modeling without requiring additional optimization tricks.\n===\n# HoliDubber: Holistic Video Dubbing for Complex Acoustic Scenes via Text-Guided Audio Synthesis\narXiv: 2606.09098\nAuthors: Wenhao Guan, Yifan Duan, Junxi Liu, Yu Gu, Feng Dang, Kaidi Wang, Qingyang Hong, Lin Li, Xie Chen\nYear: 2026 | Venue: \nTopic: \nStatus: unread\n===\n# On the Distillation Loss Functions of Speech VAE for Unified Reconstruction, Understanding, and Generation\narXiv: 2604.12383\nAuthors: Changhao Cheng, Wei Wang, Wangyou Zhang, Dongya Jia, Jian Wu, Zhuo Chen, Yanmin Qian\nYear: 2026 | Venue: \nTopic: codec_vae\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. Systematic exploration of different distillation loss functions for speech VAEs aligned with foundation models across reconstruction, understanding, and generation tasks.\n  2. Introduction of a dimension-wise distillation scheme that captures temporal structure and local feature variations.\n  3. Formulation of a joint-marginal alignment approach that enforces intra-distribution consistency between VAE latents and foundation model features via frame-level and sequence-level losses.\n  4. Development of an adaptive weighting strategy that dynamically balances reconstruction and alignment losses based on gradient norms.\n  5. Demonstration that the joint-marginal alignment with adaptive weighting achieves the best overall balance and performance across reconstruction, understanding, and generation.\n  6. Analysis revealing that frame-wise alignment primarily captures semantic information for understanding, while sequence-level alignment preserves acoustic information for reconstruction and generation.\n\nTags: 压缩, 压缩/量化-VAE, SSL", "stderr": "", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false}}
