{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-a3320f27b1594b69", "type": "tool_result", "content": "# Conditional Memory via Scalable Lookup: A New Axis of Sparsity for Large Language Models\narXiv: 2601.07372\nAuthors: Xin Cheng, Wangding Zeng, Damai Dai, Qinyu Chen, Bingxuan Wang, Zhenda Xie, Kezhao Huang, Xingkai Yu, Zhewen Hao, Yukun Li, Han Zhang, Huishuai Zhang, Dongyan Zhao, Wenfeng Liang\nYear: 2026 | Venue: \nTopic: speech_other\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. Introduces conditional memory as a complementary sparsity axis to conditional computation, instantiated via the Engram module that modernizes classic N-gram embeddings for O(1) lookups.\n  2. Formulates the Sparsity Allocation problem and uncovers a U-shaped scaling law that optimizes the trade-off between neural computation and static memory.\n  3. Demonstrates that scaling Engram to 27B parameters under this law achieves superior performance over strictly iso-parameter and iso-FLOPs MoE baselines across knowledge, reasoning, and code/math domains.\n  4. Reveals through mechanistic analyses that Engram relieves early layers from static reconstruction, effectively deepening the network for complex reasoning and freeing attention capacity for global context.\n  5. Establishes infrastructure-aware efficiency by enabling deterministic runtime prefetching, allowing massive embedding tables to be offloaded to host memory with negligible inference overhead.\n===\n# MoE-TTS: Enhancing Out-of-Domain Text Understanding for Description-based TTS via Mixture-of-Experts\narXiv: 2508.11326\nAuthors: Heyang Xue, Xuchen Song, Yu Tang, Jianyu Chen, Yanru Chen, Yang Li, Yahui Zhou\nYear: 2025 | Venue: \nTopic: tts_vc\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. We are the first to focus on the performance of description-based TTS with out-of-domain descriptions to bridge the gap between research and real-world applications.\n  2. MoE-TTS is the first work to apply mixture-of-experts techniques to enhance TTS models by leveraging the pre-trained knowledge and text understanding capabilities of textual LLMs.\n  3. Our design surpasses state-of-the-art commercial models in generating speech that is more aligned with both in-domain and out-of-domain descriptions.\n\nTags: TTS/架构-AR-LLM, TTS/控制-NL描述, TTS/控制-指令合成", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "6fe9f359-dca2-4a37-92e1-5115245d58de", "uuid": "5062e5d5-20ef-4282-954a-414bb1457f5c", "timestamp": "2026-08-04T09:32:27.084Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": "# Conditional Memory via Scalable Lookup: A New Axis of Sparsity for Large Language Models\narXiv: 2601.07372\nAuthors: Xin Cheng, Wangding Zeng, Damai Dai, Qinyu Chen, Bingxuan Wang, Zhenda Xie, Kezhao Huang, Xingkai Yu, Zhewen Hao, Yukun Li, Han Zhang, Huishuai Zhang, Dongyan Zhao, Wenfeng Liang\nYear: 2026 | Venue: \nTopic: speech_other\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. Introduces conditional memory as a complementary sparsity axis to conditional computation, instantiated via the Engram module that modernizes classic N-gram embeddings for O(1) lookups.\n  2. Formulates the Sparsity Allocation problem and uncovers a U-shaped scaling law that optimizes the trade-off between neural computation and static memory.\n  3. Demonstrates that scaling Engram to 27B parameters under this law achieves superior performance over strictly iso-parameter and iso-FLOPs MoE baselines across knowledge, reasoning, and code/math domains.\n  4. Reveals through mechanistic analyses that Engram relieves early layers from static reconstruction, effectively deepening the network for complex reasoning and freeing attention capacity for global context.\n  5. Establishes infrastructure-aware efficiency by enabling deterministic runtime prefetching, allowing massive embedding tables to be offloaded to host memory with negligible inference overhead.\n===\n# MoE-TTS: Enhancing Out-of-Domain Text Understanding for Description-based TTS via Mixture-of-Experts\narXiv: 2508.11326\nAuthors: Heyang Xue, Xuchen Song, Yu Tang, Jianyu Chen, Yanru Chen, Yang Li, Yahui Zhou\nYear: 2025 | Venue: \nTopic: tts_vc\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. We are the first to focus on the performance of description-based TTS with out-of-domain descriptions to bridge the gap between research and real-world applications.\n  2. MoE-TTS is the first work to apply mixture-of-experts techniques to enhance TTS models by leveraging the pre-trained knowledge and text understanding capabilities of textual LLMs.\n  3. Our design surpasses state-of-the-art commercial models in generating speech that is more aligned with both in-domain and out-of-domain descriptions.\n\nTags: TTS/架构-AR-LLM, TTS/控制-NL描述, TTS/控制-指令合成", "stderr": "", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false}}
